

[38;5;231m⏺[39m I explored the repo first: it's a minimal CommonJS Node project ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, a bare [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with
  no dependencies), no React, no build tooling, no tests.

  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m A React todo list is a new project inside this repo — there's no existing UI flow to
  modify, so this gets the full treatment: clarifying questions, approaches, a design you approve, then a written spec.
  Say the word if you'd rather I treat it as a quick throwaway spike instead.

  First question, and it drives almost everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much app this is. The difference isn't a feature count — it's whether state lives in one component or
  needs a real shape, and whether we need persistence and routing at all.

  - [1mMinimal[0m — add, toggle, delete, in-memory. State is one [38;5;153museState[39m array in one component. Cheap to build, cheap to
    throw away. Adding persistence later is easy; adding filters/edit later means reworking the component.
  - [1mStandard[0m — the above plus edit-in-place, filter (all/active/done), clear-completed, and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m persistence.
    Needs a reducer and a couple of components, but no backend. This is the shape most "todo list" requests actually
    mean.
  - [1mFull[0m — multiple lists, due dates, tags, sorting. Needs a real data model and probably routing. Meaningfully more
    work; hard to trim back down afterward.

  [1mMy recommendation: Standard.[0m Minimal is genuinely finishable in one sitting but you'd likely immediately want filters
  and persistence, and retrofitting those is the expensive direction. Full is speculative until you tell me it isn't.
